A||||5||JAMIE||cleaning seat belts||||1||
Z||000000||JAMIE||10-03-2000||11:14 AM||||Anyone ever cleaned up their seat belts?What is the best way to clean them?Type of cleaner?<P>Jamie||||reg||1
Z||000001||ROB MAC||10-03-2000||01:20 PM||||I put HOT water and laundry detergent in a small pail. Enough to make it sudsy. Take a stiff nylon bristle brush and dip it in the suds ( don't soak it ) and start scrubbing. When it dries it should be clean unless you got something like paint on them.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfixit58||10-03-2000||02:45 PM||||I cleaned a set that came in my 69 Firebird. They were filthy... dirt, bondo dust, grime, and who knows what else. I put a couple of gallons of hot water and about a cap full of Dawn dish water soap (cuts oil and grease like majic) into a bucket and soaked them for about an hour. I then removed them and used a stiff scrub brush on them. I then rinsed them until the soap was gone.<P>Once rinsed, I layed them in the sun to dry (important) naturally. By allowing them to dry in the sun, the nylon retuned to it's original softness. If you take them inside to dry the belts stiffened. The belts came out great and looked and felt almost like new.<P>For the Fiero, be sure to keep the retractor dry. Usually, only the portion that is regularly handled will be dirty so there should be no need to submerge the retractor. You may want to pull the belt out and use a large binding clamp, like you use for large packets of paper, to keep it from retracting. Maybe a plastic bag over the mechanisum for good measure.<P>Good Luck,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||JAMIE||10-03-2000||05:02 PM||||Thanks for the help guys.<P>Jamie||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||10-04-2000||01:00 PM||||There're several methods - try: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/004615.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/004615.html</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTZ34||10-06-2000||12:11 PM||||Hey go with the best. I use "Lifter 1" as seen on Motor Trend (you know they wouldn't lie...).  That stuff works like a dry cleaner except you spray it on wet to soak down to the fibers, only let it sit 1 minute, blot excess and then let it dry and vacuum. I "discovered" it at Wal-Mart and it has never failed me yet, grease, soda, coffee whatever.  I also used it to clean my Fiero seat belt on the driver side. I think the previous owner must have been a grease monkey (and a bad one at that...)<BR>Good luck!<P>------------------<BR>87GTZ34<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 10-06-2000).]||||reg||12
